The [4471 page](/kb/incidents/4471) says one issue, one fix. Reading the 412 merged reports
(one id, one report, as the desk counts them) against the tenant directory shows two more
problems merged in, neither with a ticket of its own. Neither is fixed by the queued parser
fix.

<Callout kind="warn">
The parser ticket is really two causes: 33 of its 412 reports are the v3 form's date hint,
and 5 read as one-day moves — 38 reports of two other bugs, merged in with no ticket of
their own. "One issue, one fix" is false.
</Callout>

## 1 · The v3 form's date hint (33 reports)

`booking-v3` prints **MM/DD/YYYY** under its date field, but every v3 tenant writes day
first: alder.physio (12 reports), copper.studio (11), pale.harbour (10). Their people type
what the hint tells them, month first, and the form reads it the tenant's way: day first.
The date comes out flipped — the same symptom, a different cause.

15 of the 33 were tagged `parser` at intake; 18 `hint`. Intake guessed from the browser's
locale, which says day first, while the form says month first — and intake never looks the
tenant directory up. Seen 2026-04-17 → 2026-09-22, all in the `form` channel, all
booking-v3 tenants.

The queued parser fix will not fix these: the flip is the hint's fault, not the parser's.
They need their own ticket, and `booking-v3` should print the hint in the tenant's own
date order (or none at all).

## 2 · One-day moves (5 reports)

Five of the 412 read as one-day moves, not month flips:

- R-41202 kettle.books — "Invite says Tuesday 12 May. Calendar says Monday 11 May."
- R-41209 alder.physio — "Every booking in June is one day off. Only June."
- R-41236 nine.lanterns — "The invite arrived before I made the booking."
- R-41344 pale.harbour — "Client in Lisbon got Tuesday. Client in Toronto got Monday."
- R-41620 lantern.row — "Tuesday 6 October showed as Monday."

The bodies point at more than one mechanism (a one-day move, a timezone, a queue race), so
they are not the parser bug, and the parser fix will not fix them. All five were tagged
`parser` at intake; the desk read them one-day in desk-notes.json, footnoted under the
parser group on the 4471 page, counted where filed — but they never got a ticket of their
own.

